# OCR caches, not results. `pagetext/` is the per-page text of every PDF and
# `parts/` and `raw/` are per-file intermediates — together several megabytes of
# machine output that any of the extractors will regenerate. The JSON beside
# them is the result and is tracked, because a batch that cites a page number
# should be checkable against what the page actually said.
pagetext/
parts/
raw/
__pycache__/
status-*.json

# Rendered pages. The answer-key recovery rasterises every page of every
# end-of-module paper to find highlights that exist only as pixels; the images
# are a cache, regenerable from the PDFs, and megabytes of them.
*.png
*.ppm
*.jpg

# The end-of-module answer-key recovery caches two kinds of intermediate under
# `eom-renders/`: page rasters, already covered by the image rules above, and
# the per-page tesseract TSV that supplies the option boxes. Both regenerate
# from the PDFs — the rasters in about a second a page, the OCR in about two —
# and together they run to gigabytes. `eom-answers.json` is the result and is
# tracked; these are not.
eom-renders/
pagetext-eom/
pagetext-eom/

# The 108 INT lane caches its renders in a directory rather than loose files,
# so the extension rules above do not reach them.
108-INT/renders/

# Two more 108 INT caches, same reasoning as `pagetext/` above. The practical
# atlas's text layer is undecodable so its pages are cached OCR'd, and the
# pharmacology handout's PDF highlight annotations are cached as extracted
# quads. Both rebuild from the corpus PDFs with `--force`; the JSON beside
# them is the result and is tracked.
108-INT/pagetext-ocr/
108-INT/highlights/
sitting-rows/
